            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A type of the grave (Sheol) and an instrument of death, burial, and divine preservation leading to resurrection, most notably functioning as the 'Sign of Jonah' pointing to Christ's entombment.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17\",\"Jonah 2:2\",\"Matthew 12:40\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Cosmic chaos, primordial evil, and Satanic forces hostile to God's created order (often conceptualized as Leviathan or Rahab)\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 27:1\",\"Psalm 74:13-14\",\"Job 41:1-34\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"Hostile, oppressive pagan empires (such as Egypt or Babylon) that devour or swallow up God's people\",\"basis\":[\"Jeremiah 51:34\",\"Ezekiel 29:3\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"An instrument of God's sovereign providence, judgment, and mercy, deployed to correct and save His prophet\",\"basis\":[\"Jonah 1:17\",\"Jonah 2:10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolism bifurcates slightly based on the specific biblical vocabulary. The 'great fish' of Jonah is primarily treated typologically as the grave and a vehicle for resurrection. In contrast, 'sea-monsters' (such as Leviathan, Rahab, or Tannin) heavily draw on Ancient Near Eastern chaos motifs to represent demonic forces, untamed nature, or tyrannical anti-God empires.\"}",
